---
# IS THE WORKER PIPELINE STILL MOVING? -- the five questions the queue can fail in, and none of
# them is "is the queue big".
#
# The first three ask whether the queue is MOVING (running away, claimed by nobody, or gone with
# the process). The last two ask whether the movement ACHIEVES anything, which is the failure that
# looks healthiest from outside: a pool at full throughput, re-failing or discarding everything it
# claims, with a flat queue depth and every other rule here silent.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY. `kinowo-worker-queue-backlog` (a Grafana rule, retired alongside
# this file) fired on `queue_depth{state="waiting"} > 150`. Measured over the 24h before it was
# removed: the US worker's MEDIAN waiting depth is 450 and it spent 98.1% of the day above that
# threshold. It was firing continuously, and had been for as long as the US roster had been that
# size. A permanently-firing alert is worse than no alert -- it is the thing that teaches you to
# scroll past the channel where the real one will appear.
#
# THE DEPTH WAS NEVER THE SIGNAL. A queue of 450 that drains at 143 tasks/min is healthy; a queue
# of 20 that nothing is claiming is not. What matters is the DERIVATIVE (is it running away) and
# the THROUGHPUT (is anything being claimed at all), and both are independent of how big the
# backlog happens to be on a roster of 5,000 US venues.
#
# EVERY THRESHOLD BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST 24h OF THE FLEET'S OWN DATA rather than guessed, and
# the measurement is written beside each one so the next person can redo it instead of arguing.
groups:
  - name: worker-pipeline
    rules:
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 1. THE QUEUE IS RUNNING AWAY -- a slope that stays positive for a solid hour.
      #
      # WHY A SLOPE AND NOT A LEVEL: see the header. `deriv` over 1h is the "trend line", and the
      # `for: 1h` is the "continuously" -- the condition has to hold for a full hour of evaluation,
      # so an ordinary enqueue burst that drains cannot reach it.
      #
      # 0.05 tasks/sec IS 180 TASKS/HOUR OF NET GROWTH, and it is chosen from the data. Longest
      # CONTINUOUS run above each candidate over 24h, per country:
      #
      #     > 0      de 65m  es 55m  pl 35m  uk 50m  us 50m   <- `for: 1h` would have fired on DE
      #     > 0.02   de  0   es  0   pl  0   uk 10m  us 35m
      #     > 0.05   de  0   es  0   pl  0   uk  0   us 25m   <- 35 minutes of headroom
      #     > 0.10   de  0   es  0   pl  0   uk  0   us  5m
      #
      # The queue oscillates by design (reapers enqueue in bursts, the pool drains them), so a
      # naive `deriv > 0` is true about half of any given day -- which is exactly why the obvious
      # rule is the wrong one.
      - alert: WorkerQueueGrowingUnbounded
        expr: deriv(kinowo_worker_queue_depth{state="waiting"}[1h]) > 0.05
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s task queue has grown for an hour straight ({{ $value | printf \"%.3f\" }} tasks/s)"
          description: >-
            The WAITING set has climbed continuously for an hour at more than 180 tasks/hour net,
            which is a queue the pool is not keeping up with rather than a burst it is working
            through. THE DEPTH ITSELF IS NOT THE PROBLEM and is not worth reacting to -- the US
            worker sits at ~450 waiting in normal operation. Look at what is ENQUEUEING: a reaper
            that has started scheduling faster than the pool can drain (panels 20/30 on the
            application dashboard split enqueued from worked), or a task type failing and being
            retried forever (panel 25, waiting by type). `WorkerQueueStalled` below is the other
            half of this: if nothing is being claimed at all, that one fires instead and is the
            more urgent of the two.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 2. NOTHING IS BEING CLAIMED, AND THERE IS WORK TO CLAIM. The pipeline has stopped.
      #
      # THE OBVIOUS FORM OF THIS RULE IS UNUSABLE, and it is worth recording why so nobody
      # "simplifies" it back. `queue_depth{state="worked_on"} == 0 and waiting > 0` reads like the
      # same statement and is true 39-84% of any given day (UK 83.7%) -- because `worked_on` is an
      # instantaneous gauge and tasks finish in milliseconds, so a scrape lands between claims more
      # often than not. It would page every few minutes for a perfectly healthy worker.
      #
      # THE THROUGHPUT FORM IS EXACT: no task has been CLAIMED in five minutes while the queue was
      # non-empty. Measured over 24h across all five countries, this condition was true for
      # 0.0000% of the time -- it has no false-positive rate to speak of, because a healthy worker
      # claims 13-143 tasks a minute.
      #
      # IT IS UP TO TEN MINUTES OF SILENCE BEFORE THIS FIRES, not five: the `rate` window and the
      # `for:` are five minutes EACH and they run in series -- the rate has to fall to zero, which
      # takes five minutes of no claims, and only then does the `for:` start counting. Both halves
      # earn their length (a shorter rate window on a 15s scrape is noisy, and a shorter `for:`
      # would fire on the gap around a rollout), so the fix is to say ten rather than to shorten
      # one of them. The summary said "5 minutes" until 2026-09-06 and was wrong about it.
      #
      # A COLD START CAN REACH IT. `tasks_started_total` resets on restart, so a worker that is
      # being scraped but has not claimed anything for ten minutes -- a long hydrate, a pool
      # waiting on Mongo -- fires this even though nothing is broken yet. That is arguably the
      # right answer (ten minutes past boot with work waiting IS a stall) and the description says
      # where to look, but the measured 0.0000% is a steady-state number and does not cover it.
      #
      # CRITICAL, unlike the growth alert: a queue growing slowly is a capacity conversation, a
      # queue nobody is touching is the pipeline stopped -- scrapes, enrichment and the read-model
      # projection all ride this queue.
      - alert: WorkerQueueStalled
        expr: >-
          (sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_tasks_started_total[5m])) == 0)
          and on (country)
          (max by (country) (kinowo_worker_queue_depth{state="waiting"}) > 0)
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s worker has claimed no task for ten minutes with work waiting"
          description: >-
            Tasks are queued and NOTHING is picking them up, so every scrape, rating refresh and
            read-model projection for this country has stopped. A healthy worker claims 13-143
            tasks a minute. Check, in order: is the pod up and past its hydrate (`WorkerDown` fires
            separately if the target is gone); is the whole pool stuck on handlers that never
            return (`kinowo_worker_queue_depth{state="worked_on"}` pinned at the pool size of 4 is
            that shape, and the task-duration panels name the type); and is `TaskQueue.claim`
            failing in the logs -- a Mongo stall shows up here first because claim is a
            `findOneAndUpdate` that blocks.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 3. THE WORKER ITSELF IS GONE.
      #
      # `TargetDown` in monitoring-self.rules already fires on `up == 0` for ANY job at 10m; this
      # is the same condition at 5m for the workers specifically, because they are the pipeline and
      # ten minutes of silence is ten minutes of no scraping.
      #
      # IT DOES NOT INHIBIT `TargetDown`, and this comment claimed it did until 2026-09-06. Both are
      # `severity: critical`, and the critical-silences-warning rule only reaches warnings; the
      # host-scoped rule requires `job = "node"` on its source and a worker's `TargetDown` carries
      # `job = "kinowo-worker"`. So a dead worker really is two messages, five minutes apart. That
      # is tolerable -- they say the same thing and the second confirms the first -- and it is not
      # worth an inhibit rule whose source and target are both critical, which is the shape
      # Alertmanager's own documentation warns against.
      #
      # 5 MINUTES SURVIVES A DEPLOY, and that is measured, not assumed: over 24h the workers
      # restarted 23-26 times each and the LONGEST continuous stretch any of them was unscrapeable
      # was 2.5 minutes (us; de/es/uk 1.5, pl 2.0). Half the window is headroom. A shorter `for:`
      # would page on every rollout, which on this fleet is roughly hourly.
      - alert: WorkerDown
        expr: up{job="kinowo-worker"} == 0
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "The {{ $labels.country }} worker has been unreachable for 5 minutes"
          description: >-
            Prometheus cannot scrape this worker, so it is either crash-looping, wedged, or gone.
            Nothing is scraping cinemas, refreshing ratings or projecting the read model for
            {{ $labels.country }} while this holds. A rollout takes it down for under three minutes,
            so five means something other than a deploy. `kubectl -n kinowo logs deploy/worker-{{ $labels.country }}`
            and the JVM heap panels are the first two places to look -- an OOM kill presents exactly
            like this.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 4. THE QUEUE IS MOVING AND GETTING NOWHERE -- tasks are being claimed and finished, and a
      # quarter of them are coming back as failures or retries.
      #
      # THIS IS THE HOLE THE THREE RULES ABOVE LEAVE, and rule 1's own description points straight
      # at it: "a task type failing and being retried forever". Such a loop claims tasks (so
      # `WorkerQueueStalled` stays silent), re-enqueues them at the rate it drains them (so the
      # depth is FLAT and `WorkerQueueGrowingUnbounded` stays silent), and keeps the process up (so
      # `WorkerDown` stays silent). The pipeline spins at full throughput and the listings go stale
      # anyway, which is the failure nobody would be told about.
      #
      # 25% IS ~8x THE WORST THING THE FLEET HAS EVER DONE. Share of finishes that are `failed` or
      # `rescheduled`, measured over 24h across all five countries:
      #
      #     median   0.000 everywhere
      #     p95      0.000 everywhere except pl (0.007)
      #     max      0.029 (pl), 0.000-0.004 elsewhere
      #     longest continuous run above 0.20 / 0.50 / 0.80:  0 minutes, every country, both outcomes
      #
      # In absolute terms the whole fleet finished 464,903 tasks in that day and 13 of them failed.
      # `deferred` is deliberately NOT counted: it means a circuit breaker refused the task untried,
      # which is a host being unreachable rather than the pipeline eating itself, and it is already
      # separated from `rescheduled` in the metric for exactly that reason.
      #
      # THE SECOND CLAUSE IS A FLOOR ON THE DENOMINATOR. A ratio is meaningless when almost nothing
      # is finishing: a country that completes three tasks in the window, one of which retries, is
      # at 33% and perfectly healthy. Requiring 0.01 failures/sec (36 an hour) means the alert
      # describes a loop rather than an arithmetic accident.
      #
      # `deferred` IS EXCLUDED FROM THE DENOMINATOR TOO, and leaving it in was a bug for one day.
      # Excluding it from the numerator alone means a circuit-breaker storm -- thousands of tasks
      # refused untried, all landing in the denominator -- DILUTES the ratio and suppresses the
      # alert at exactly the moment the pipeline is spinning hardest and achieving least.
      #
      # `skipped` STAYS IN, and the distinction is not "was the work attempted" -- on that reading
      # `skipped` (data already fresh) would come out too. It is which way the outcome MOVES with
      # the failure this alert is looking for. `deferred` spikes WITH it: the same upstream outage
      # that opens a circuit is what makes handlers throw beside it, so counting it lets the
      # symptom hide the disease. `skipped` is the healthy-idle baseline and moves the other way;
      # dropping it would put a quiet country with two retries at a ratio of 1.0 and leave the
      # whole alert leaning on the absolute floor below.
      - alert: WorkerTasksFailingRepeatedly
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_tasks_finished_total{outcome=~"failed|rescheduled"}[15m]))
            /
            sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_tasks_finished_total{outcome!="deferred"}[15m]))
          ) > 0.25
          and on (country)
          sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_tasks_finished_total{outcome=~"failed|rescheduled"}[15m])) > 0.01
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s worker is failing or retrying {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of the tasks it finishes"
          description: >-
            The pool is busy and the work is not sticking. Over 24h of normal operation this share
            is 0.000 in every country -- 13 failures in 464,903 tasks -- so a quarter is a loop, not
            a bad afternoon. Panel 25 (waiting by type) and the per-type finish counters name WHICH
            type: `sum by (task_type) (rate(kinowo_worker_tasks_finished_total{outcome=~"failed|rescheduled"}[15m]))`.
            The usual causes are an upstream that has started refusing us (the scraper types), a
            handler throwing on a data shape it has not seen, and a task whose precondition can
            never be met so it reschedules itself forever. NOTE `deferred` IS EXCLUDED: a circuit
            breaker refusing tasks is an upstream being down and reads on the scrape dashboards.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 5. A TASK TYPE NOBODY HANDLES. Tasks are being claimed, dropped on the floor, and counted.
      #
      # `no_handler` is what `TaskWorker` records when it claims a task and finds nothing wired to
      # run it. It is not a degradation, it is a WIRING BUG: whatever enqueued those tasks believes
      # the work is happening, and the queue drains them as fast as it can to no effect. The
      # measured baseline is exactly zero -- 105 series, every one 0 over 24h -- so any occurrence
      # at all is the alert, with no threshold to argue about.
      #
      # `for: 5m` DELAYS THIS BY FIVE MINUTES AND SUPPRESSES NOTHING, which is worth stating plainly
      # because the first version of this comment claimed it "rides out a deploy". It cannot:
      # `increase(...[1h])` stays above zero for a full hour after a single dropped task, so the
      # `for:` is guaranteed to elapse. One task discarded during a rollout fires this and holds it
      # for about sixty-five minutes.
      #
      # THAT IS THE INTENDED ANSWER, not a tolerated one. A task claimed with no handler is work
      # that silently did not happen; one of them is a bug worth a message, and the hour-long tail
      # is what stops the alert flapping as the flow pauses and resumes. The five minutes buys a
      # rolling deploy the chance to finish before the mail goes out, and nothing more.
      - alert: WorkerTaskTypeUnhandled
        expr: sum by (country, task_type) (increase(kinowo_worker_tasks_finished_total{outcome="no_handler"}[1h])) > 0
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} is claiming {{ $labels.task_type }} tasks with no handler wired for them"
          description: >-
            Something is enqueueing `{{ $labels.task_type }}` and nothing is running it -- the tasks
            are claimed, counted, and discarded, so the work silently never happens while every
            throughput metric looks healthy. The baseline for this counter is zero across the whole
            fleet. Look at the handler registration in `WorkerWiring` for this type, and at whatever
            enqueues it: either the registration was lost in a rename or the enqueue outlived the
            handler it was written for.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 5b. A TASK DROPPED ON A DETERMINISTIC FAILURE. `permanent` is what `TaskWorker` records when
      # a handler throws a bare `IllegalArgumentException` -- a violated `require` -- and it drops the
      # task on that first attempt instead of retrying a failure every retry would replay (ES "La
      # luz", 2026-09-21: `rekey requires same normalised cleanTitle`, 12 identical attempts over
      # 1h45m). It is a code or data bug, so the baseline is zero and one is worth a message --
      # `warning`, like its siblings, never a page. `WorkerTasksFailingRepeatedly` cannot see it: it
      # is a RATIO, and one dropped task among hundreds finishing `done` never moves it.
      #
      # Same shape as rule 5 and for the same reason: `increase(...[1h])` holds the alert for an
      # hour after the drop, which is the intended answer (no flapping); the `for: 5m` only delays
      # the message. `increase` absorbs the counter reset a worker restart causes, so a restart
      # neither clears it nor fires it.
      - alert: WorkerTasksFailingPermanently
        expr: sum by (country, task_type) (increase(kinowo_worker_tasks_finished_total{outcome="permanent"}[1h])) > 0
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} dropped {{ $labels.task_type }} task(s) on a deterministic failure"
          description: >-
            A `{{ $labels.task_type }}` handler threw a bare IllegalArgumentException (a violated
            `require`), so `TaskWorker` dropped the task on its first attempt rather than retrying a
            failure every retry would replay. That is a bug in the code or in the task's data, and the
            work did not happen. The worker log line "failed permanently on attempt" names the task's
            dedup key and the message; a reaper that still finds the work due will re-enqueue it and
            hit the same failure until the bug is fixed.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # 5c. A TASK PARKED PAST THE BACKOFF CAP. The queue holds a task back after a failure (the
      # retry backoff, capped at 30 minutes), after a Deferred (until the instant its handler names,
      # clamped to the same cap), and a staggered chunk until its slot (at most 5 minutes). The
      # head-of-line age leaves every held-back task out on purpose, so a task parked for hours --
      # the "parked in backoff for ever" class -- sat on no gauge at all.
      # `kinowo_worker_queue_parked_max_seconds` is the longest REMAINING hold per (country, type);
      # every path that sets a hold is bounded by the cap, so its healthy ceiling is 1800 and
      # anything above it is a hold nothing in the code should have produced.
      #
      # EXCEPT RescrapeShareCard, which is not a hold but a SCHEDULE: each Facebook re-scrape is
      # enqueued at the next slot 10 seconds after the last one (ShareCardService.RescrapeSpacing,
      # so the Graph API never sees a burst), and a template change re-draws every recent film at
      # once — 200 films put the last slot 33 minutes out, and a large country hours. It is left
      # out here rather than capped, since capping it would bring back the burst.
      #
      # 1860 is the cap plus a minute for the gap between the pod that released the task and the
      # pod that samples it. `max by` because every worker pod of a country samples the same queue.
      # The hold falls one second per second, so `for: 2m` fires only for a task parked at least
      # 2m past the cap and rides over a single odd sample.
      - alert: WorkerTaskParkedTooLong
        expr: max by (country, task_type) (kinowo_worker_queue_parked_max_seconds{task_type!="RescrapeShareCard"}) > 1860
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} has a {{ $labels.task_type }} task parked past the 30-minute backoff cap"
          description: >-
            A waiting `{{ $labels.task_type }}` task is held back from claim for another
            {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, past the 30-minute cap every retry backoff, Deferred and
            chunk stagger is held to (TaskWorker.MaxBackoff). Nothing will run it until then. Find it on
            the worker's /tasks page (its next-eligible time is the far one) and look at what released
            it: a handler naming a far-off instant through a path that skips the clamp, or a queue
            write setting `nextEligibleAt` directly.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE COMPANION. Rules 1 and 2 are comparisons against a series, and a comparison cannot fire
      # when its series is gone -- so a worker that stops publishing its queue gauges would leave
      # both silent, which is indistinguishable from a queue that is behaving. `WorkerDown` covers
      # the case where the whole target vanishes; this covers the narrower one where the process is
      # up and answering /metrics without the queue family in it.
      - alert: WorkerQueueMetricsAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_worker_queue_depth)
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No worker is publishing task-queue metrics"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_queue_depth` has no series from any country, so neither
            WorkerQueueGrowingUnbounded nor WorkerQueueStalled can fire and the pipeline is
            unwatched. If the workers were simply down, `WorkerDown` would be firing too and is the
            better alert to read; if they are up, the queue gauges have stopped being refreshed
            from the per-country `QueueSnapshot`, which no target-level check would catch.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # A REPOSITORY WRITE THAT KEEPS FAILING. The queue can be moving and every task finishing
      # `done` while none of it reaches Mongo: on 2026-09-24 a codec bug made `movies` upserts and
      # `movie_slots` writes throw for ~6h (34 failures, 29 films). Each was caught, logged at WARN
      # and returned as if nothing happened, and two new films never reached the site until a
      # restart. `kinowo_worker_repository_write_failed_total` is that failure, counted where the
      # repository catches it (services.movies.RepositoryWrite); its baseline is zero.
      #
      # SUSTAINED, NOT ANY: a failed write rolls its row out of the cache so the next scrape
      # retries it, and a single blip (a stepdown, a timeout) heals that way on its own. `[5m]`
      # with `for: 10m` needs failures in every five-minute window for ten minutes running.
      #
      # RESTART-GAP-SAFE the way `last_over_time(...[10m])` makes the gauges elsewhere: a pod
      # change leaves the series absent for ~3 minutes, and `increase(...[5m])` still holds the
      # old pod's samples across that hole while the new pod's series starts, so the `for:` is
      # not reset by a rollout. The worker seeds every known (collection, op) at 0 for the
      # expected exception classes, so `increase` counts from the FIRST failure; a class outside
      # that list appears on its first failure and counts from its second.
      - alert: RepositoryWritesFailing
        expr: sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_repository_write_failed_total[5m])) > 0
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s worker has been failing repository writes for 10 minutes"
          description: >-
            Writes to `movies`, `movie_slots`, `screenings` or `pending_movies` are throwing.
            Nothing is lost silently -- each failed row is rolled out of the worker's cache and
            retried by its next scrape -- but until the cause clears, new films and changed
            showtimes do not reach the site. The worker's WARN lines name each write
            (`MovieRepository.upsert(<title>, <year>) failed: ...`), and the counter's
            `collection`, `op` and `exception` labels (worker diagnostics dashboard, "Repository
            writes that FAILED") say which store and why: a codec exception is a model field the
            DTO cannot encode, a MongoWriteException a validator or duplicate key, a timeout the
            database itself.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # A TASK TYPE WHOSE HEAD HAS WAITED HOURS. `WorkerQueueStalled` fires only when NOTHING is
      # claimed, and `WorkerQueueGrowingUnbounded` only when depth climbs. Neither sees one type
      # starved behind the others while the queue as a whole moves: a resolve or a scrape that is
      # claimable and never claimed, so a film is never resolved or a cinema never re-scraped, and
      # every throughput panel looks healthy.
      #
      # THE GAUGE IS ALREADY THE RIGHT QUESTION. `kinowo_worker_queue_oldest_waiting_age_seconds`
      # is the age of the oldest CLAIMABLE waiting task per type -- tasks held in retry backoff or
      # scheduled for later are excluded where it is computed (WorkerTaskMetrics), so deferred work
      # does not inflate it and a long backoff cannot page.
      #
      # TWO HOURS, HELD HALF AN HOUR. Measured over the whole retention (30 days to 2026-09-24):
      # the per-country maxima were ES 1.7h (ResolveTmdb, 09-21), UK 1.2h, US 0.5h, DE 0.25h, PL
      # 0.18h. An hour -- the first draft -- would have fired on the ES 09-21 peak, a backlog that
      # drained by itself; two hours sits above everything the fleet has done while healthy and
      # far below the day-long head PL once reached. Zero firings over the 30 days.
      #
      # `last_over_time(...[10m])` BRIDGES A WORKER RESTART, as the coverage guard requires for a
      # held gauge; a restart re-seeds the gauge at 0, so a real restart clears it honestly.
      - alert: WorkerQueueHeadOld
        expr: >-
          max by (country, task_type) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_queue_oldest_waiting_age_seconds[10m])) > 2 * 3600
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s oldest claimable {{ $labels.task_type }} task has waited {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            A {{ $labels.task_type }} task in "{{ $labels.country }}" has been claimable -- due, not
            in backoff -- for over two hours and nothing has picked it up, while the rest of the
            queue may well be moving. Either the pool is saturated by other types (the queue-depth
            panel by type, and `kinowo_worker_queue_depth{state="worked_on"}` pinned at the pool
            size) or claim ordering is starving this type. The country's /tasks page lists the
            waiting tasks with their due times; the oldest one is the one this is about.

      # A read-model scan SKIPPED a document it could not decode. The skip is deliberate -- one
      # corrupt document must not sink the keyset page it arrived in -- but the skipped document
      # is a film or screening the reader goes without, and it used to be a WARN line and nothing
      # else. Any skip in the hour is worth a look: the healthy reading is exactly zero.
      - alert: DocumentsUndecodable
        expr: >-
          sum by (country, collection) (increase(kinowo_worker_decode_failures_total[1h])) > 0
          or
          sum by (country, collection) (increase(kinowo_web_decode_failures_total[1h])) > 0
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: documents in {{ $labels.collection }} could not be decoded"
          description: >-
            A full scan of `{{ $labels.collection }}` skipped at least one document in the last hour
            because it could not be decoded, so the process that read it serves or projects without
            that film or screening. The WARN line names each one ("skipping undecodable document
            _id=..."), with the exception; the worker diagnostics dashboard ("Documents skipped as
            UNDECODABLE") says whether the worker, the web or both saw it. A wrong-typed field is
            usually a writer that changed shape: the projector's reconcile rewrites the document
            once the writer is fixed. In `movies` nothing is skipped: every read that meets the
            document FAILS (its film's point read, and the whole corpus scan), and the WARN line is
            that read's failure — fix or remove the document by hand. A change stream that meets
            one skips that event ("skipping undecodable document", rate-limited) and stays open.

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # IS EVERYTHING THE WORKER SHOULD BE DOING ACTUALLY WIRED? The in-app Telegram alerters and the
  # external integrations are each built from `Env.get(...)` in a for-comprehension, so a missing
  # env var is not an error -- it is a `None`, and the feature is simply not there. When the
  # workers moved from Fly to k3s the Telegram chat ids were left behind and all three alerters
  # (Filmweb fallback, Filmweb drops, staging stuck) were OFF for weeks with nothing noticing. The
  # worker now publishes 1/0 per feature (`EnvGatedFeature`), and the WARN line at boot names the
  # key: `kubectl -n kinowo logs deploy/worker-<country> | grep 'is OFF: missing'`.
  #
  # ZERO IS ONLY EVER A MISCONFIGURATION IN PRODUCTION, so this is `== 0`, not a tuned threshold:
  # every key is set on every worker (worker-env / worker-secrets), and a Filmweb alerter is not
  # exported at all for a country without Filmweb, so "off by design" never reads 0.
  #
  # THIRTY MINUTES, THROUGH `last_over_time(...[10m])`: the value is set once at boot and never
  # changes, so the hold only has to outlast a rollout -- and the 10m window carries the reading
  # across the ~3 minutes a restart leaves the gauge absent, so a crash-looping worker cannot keep
  # resetting the hold. `min by` survives the pod being scraped twice across a relabel.
  #
  # NOT REPLAYED against history: the gauges are new in this change, so there is no history to
  # replay. The first 30 minutes after the rollout is the replay -- by the live config (checked
  # 2026-09-25) every key is present once movies-gitops a94ebb6 is applied, so it should be silent.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - name: worker-env-gated-features
    rules:
      - alert: WorkerAlerterDisabled
        expr: min by (country, alerter) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_alerter_enabled[10m])) == 0
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: the worker's {{ $labels.alerter }} Telegram alerter is switched off"
          description: >-
            The worker wired the `{{ $labels.alerter }}` alerter OFF because an env var it needs is
            missing, so the incidents it exists to report are reaching nobody. The worker's boot
            log names the key: `kubectl -n kinowo logs deploy/worker-{{ $labels.country }} | grep
            'is OFF: missing'`. The chat and topic ids live in the `worker-env` ConfigMap in
            movies-gitops (worker/base/all.yaml) and the bot token in `worker-secrets`; they were
            lost once already when the workers moved from Fly to k3s, and all three alerters were
            silently off for weeks.

      - alert: WorkerIntegrationDisabled
        expr: min by (country, integration) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_integration_enabled[10m])) == 0
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: the worker is running without {{ $labels.integration }}"
          description: >-
            The worker started without the `{{ $labels.integration }}` integration because its
            secret is missing, and carried on degraded rather than failing: no TMDB resolution
            (newcomers never reach the site), no OMDb tier, no residential proxy (Cloudflare-fronted
            chains fall to Zyte or fail), no Zyte fallback, no Sentry, or no Facebook re-scrape of a
            changed share card. The worker's boot log names the key: `kubectl -n kinowo logs
            deploy/worker-{{ $labels.country }} | grep 'is OFF: missing'`. The secrets are in
            `worker-secrets` (the Facebook pair in `web-secrets`), managed outside movies-gitops.
